# ── Ultron — what never belongs in the repository ─────────────────────────────
# Everything listed here is created by the app on first run. A fresh clone with
# none of it present boots straight to the setup screen, which is the intended
# first experience — so nothing here needs to be committed, and some of it must
# never be.
#
# One rule for this file, learned the expensive way: a comment goes on its OWN
# line. Git does not support trailing comments — `config/api_keys.json  # key`
# is a pattern containing spaces and a hash, it matches nothing, and it looks
# like the file is protected when it is not. Verified with `git check-ignore`.

# ── Secrets. Committing any of these publishes them. ──────────────────────────
# Your API keys, plus per-plugin credentials.
config/api_keys.json
# Everything config_manager writes beside it: the half-written temporary
# file and the copy it keeps when the config is damaged. BOTH contain the
# keys — the temporary one because it is a copy of the whole file, the kept
# one because damage is when a key is most likely to be lying in plain sight.
config/api_keys.json.*
# The dashboard's TLS private key and certificate.
config/certs/
# A LINKED WhatsApp session. Committing it hands somebody your WhatsApp account.
config/whatsapp_web/
# Google OAuth tokens (Gmail / Calendar plugins) and client secrets.
**/token*.json
**/client_secret*.json
*credentials*.json
.env
.env.*

# ── Personal data the assistant accumulates about you ─────────────────────────
memory/long_term.json
# Its timestamped backups too: same contents, different name.
memory/long_term.json.bak-*
memory/*.db

# ── Machine state, regenerated on every run ───────────────────────────────────
# Which files collect-data has already hashed.
.ultron-collect-state.json
# The full output of the last setup run.
setup-log.txt
# Used while run.bat looks for an interpreter.
.ultron-python.tmp
# The plan that was running at exit. It is this machine's work, not the project's.
state/

# ── Python build noise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# ── Virtual environments ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A .venv that stopped working is moved aside, not deleted, so it needs hiding too.
.venv/
.venv.old/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ── Editors and operating systems ─────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ── Runtime output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
logs/
downloads/
screenshots/

# ── Tool caches (added for CI: they are not part of the project) ──────────────
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ── Shipping and updating, if they ever land in the tree ────────────────────
# The restart marker belongs in the state folder; this is belt and braces
# so a mis-set path can never be published.
restart-required.json
ship_pending.json

# ── Per-machine report output (regenerated by scripts/verify.py) ─────────────
# verification-results.txt above is the project record; this folder is one
# machine's run of the instruments and changes on every invocation.
verification/
sidecar/bin/

# ── Versions of the system prompt ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every applied change to core/prompt.txt keeps the text it replaced, plus a
# changelog. It is the owner's history, not the repository's: a fresh clone starts
# with the shipped prompt and an empty history, which is the truth about it.
config/prompt_versions/
